00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:02.950 align:start position:0%
 
hi there  steve kaufman here um  i'm going to uh talk a bit today about  uh language learning  uh and the ipad  where i put it it's over there  uh because i um i'm committed to making  a speech here in a local library about  language learning in the age of the ipad  and i will be doing the same in europe  so i've been sort of doing a bit of  reading collecting my thoughts  and i would like to get your input i  think  what i'm going to say basically is that  the the ipad and i don't want to promote  one company's  product but it's kind of the  the sort of uh  most widely recognized brand name for a  new phenomenon which is these electronic  tablets that are going to enable us to  have really a very powerful computer  uh at our fingertips that theoretically  can be connected at all times to the  world to other people other people's  ideas uh other sources of information  and um  personally i think the ipad  in that sense as an electronic tablet is  absolutely revolutionary and i have been  using my ipad more and more for a  variety of things including language  learning but also for reading  and  the other day i decided that i wanted to  read a book called the rational optimist  i had heard about the book i didn't know  quite what the name was i just went to  amazon on my ipad i  searched for optimist and up came the  rational optimist by matt ridley i  immediately bought the book  for seven dollars  and read it on the sort of kindle uh  application  uh on my ipad and so within a day or two  i had the book and i had read it  it's just an example of the kind of  world we are living in  um one of the criticisms of of the  internet and constant connectivity is  that we are always prone to  jump from hyperlink to hyperlink and not  really engross ourselves in any one  subject  i think any tool you can use it however  you want people flip channels people  flip through books some people never  read books they read magazines if you  want to read a book it has become much  more convenient to do it now because any  book you think of you can buy it right  away and start reading it  now matt ridley's book the rational  optimist i bought it because there is a  fellow called freedman who keeps on  commenting on my blog about how the  world is headed for disaster and if we  don't do something we'll run out of  resources and global warming is going to  be so damaging and he just goes on and  on in this doomsday scenario and i'm  tired of listening to him i try to point  out  that  every similar prediction of doom and  gloom in the past has proven to be  inaccurate  and uh i had i was aware that there was  a book that described this in greater  detail so i got a hold of the book  very interesting book i very much  recommend that you read it  the basic theme of the book is  that it has been the  sort of constantly accelerating exchange  of goods but even more than goods of  ideas  and particularly in an environment not  of  exchanging like for like  but where  some people are specialized in some  product or some technology or some ideas  and other people are ex specialized in  others and they exchange these and  therefore they  dramatically increase  the range of products and ideas that are  available to them and this has been  accelerating and  uh ridley traces through a very  interesting sort of summary of of world  history and different civilizations and  periods of of growth in in civilization  and periods of of decline and typically  the decline was caused by an excess of  government intervention  and regulation  and other measures that prevented or  discouraged people from exchanging goods  and ideas  and the greater the freedom of exchange  and the greater the freedom of  expression  uh the better off we all become  and  you know there is this sort of uh  random  unorganized undirected spontaneous  bottom-up activity by human beings  connected with other human beings and  the larger the community the more  rapidly we come up with new ideas new  solutions to problems and so forth  uh and today  in order to progress we need a  collaborative brain collaborative  knowledge  collective brain i think he talks about  it and wherever a community  had enjoyed this kind of interaction and  then was isolated from  the rest of the world they tended to  stagnate they tended to lose skills that  they had because there were fewer fewer  people with these specialized skills  and as a result ridley points out that  uh since 1970 the world cereal harvest  has more than doubled on the same land  base and therefore if that had not  happened either we wouldn't have as many  people alive today and of course i don't  have the number but i think the  population has probably doubled during  that time as well  we would have had to use twice as much  land in order to grow  uh the same amount of food assuming that  that was even possible  it's also true  that uh according to uh d lal who must  be an indian writer that the consumption  of the poor has grown over the last 20  years  twice as fast as the world average there  has according to the united nations  there's a decline  in world poverty  so that we are more and more of us  living better and better  uh pollution i mean a car traveling at  normal speed apparently pollutes less  than a car who parked somewhere in 1970  would pollute just through leakage  so apparently everything has gone down  in cost with the exception of health and  education  and if we look at the united states the  cost of educating someone from  kindergarten to grade 12 after inflation  has increased by 350 since 1970. yet the  incarceration rate in the united states  has increased 700 percent and  educational outcomes are at best the  same or worse so we're spending three  and a half times as much money for  poorer outcomes why is this might it  have something to do with our education  system with a classroom with the  authoritarian top-down  isolated  uh  protectionist  uh  environment where we have credentials  and curricula  and and and we limit the contact  between like a lateral contact amongst  learners teachers are very resentful i  can tell from my own experience with  link very resentful of any  outsider trying to get involved in  education with new ideas so they're very  much like these isolated  uh  communities like tasmania where skills  decline  to my mind the ipad represents a new  paradigm if the school system is like a  traffic light stop go red light green  light  the ipad is like the roundabout where  people make their own decisions and  people learn for themselves in an  environment of lifelong learning they  connect with other learners they connect  with other people with whom they can  exchange  as we do at link exchange my language  for your language and not just that link  by the way at a multiplicity  of websites on the internet i mean how  can we compare the creativity of people  developing educational apps for the ipad  versus the textbooks and the  institutionalized curricula we have in  in schools with the ipad as a symbol we  now can  customize to individual needs and  requirements and the teacher's not out  of the equation because the teacher if  he gets out of the school room can  actually access far more people not just  the k to 12 but all the people who want  to learn the teacher can be a source of  motivation encouragement  uh encouraging people to get active  because what we need to measure is is  how active people are in their learning  not necessarily measure them against  some arbitrary uh some arbitrary  grammar test in the case of language  learning so in summary because i'm  running out of time here we are safer  richer healthier living longer than any  time before if things have improved so  dramatically in the last little while  why be so pessimistic about the future  we need to recognize that all the dire  predictions of running out of this that  the other have never turned out to be  true because mankind is innovative and  you can't extrapolate the past into the  future and let's take that same  creativity and exchange  and innovative attitude into education  into and in particular language  education so i'd be interested in  hearing your thoughts on  ramble you just heard

